e 1962 prints `Per Dominum nostrum Iesum Christum Filium tuum Qui tecum vivit et regnat in imitate`; the 1862 sets 18 point(s) of punctuation in this oration where the 1962 sets 10; the 1922 Mame reads `Oratio EUS` where the 1962 reads `Deus`; the 1922 Mame reads `inhzrére` where the 1962 reads `inhaerere` (letterforms inferred, not read: the difference is at the level of the recogniser); the 1922 Mame reads `héstium` where the 1962 reads `hostium` (letterforms inferred, not read: the difference is at the level of the recogniser); 3 further place(s) differ only in orthography, ligature or the recogniser's letterforms; the 1922 Mame prints no conclusion at this place; the 1962 prints `Per Dominum nostrum Iesum Christum Filium tuum Qui tecum vivit et regnat in imitate`. Corroboration: 1922 Tours Mame, printed p. 590 (artifact PDF p. 704), target-body character coverage 0.97, found by a sweep; Benziger 1962 conformed printing, target-body character coverage 0.93. Recorded limitation: The 1962 prints this under 05-05 with the rubric Commune unius aut plurium Summorum Pontificum. Every 1862 reading here rests on that book's whole-book text layer alone; this lane has no page image of the 1862, and the recogniser damages roughly one word in three there, so where the 1862's letterforms differ from the 1962's the difference recorded above is an inference from the layer and not a reading of the leaf. The 1922 Mame is the check on those places, and it is a full public-domain peer rather than mere corroboration. Not checked: the 1962 page image for this oration, the 1570 (its recogniser is too degraded to control a claim), and the 1604 except where named. || REPAIRED by lane repair-2 on 2026-09-03. The stored target had been a text-layer reading carrying recogniser damage; it has been replaced word for word by a reading taken from the 1962 CMAA facsimile page image at 400 dpi (artifact PDF p. 601, printed folio read on the same image). Line-end hyphens are joined, printed stress accents are dropped, and the ae/oe ligatures are expanded to two letters; punctuation, capitalisation and the conclusion are reproduced at the length the page prints them. The verdict, the antecedent locus and the recorded differences are the original lane's and were not revisited. The marginal number 2517 was read on the same 400 dpi image and is added to the locus, which previously carried none.","relationship":"editorial-projection-exact-to-target","source_date":"2026-09-03 project editorial projection; antecedent Pustet Ratisbon 1862","source_id":"passage.triptych.roman-1962-latin-proper-editorial-projection.editorial-projection-2026-09-03.s-pii-v-papae-confessoris-collect","status":"collated","transformations":["join printed line wraps","remove stress accents","expand printed ae/oe ligatures to ae/oe","normalise consonantal i to the calendar's j orthography","apply the target printing's punctuation","carry the target's conclusion at its printed length"],"verification_locator":"printed p. 520, marginal no. 2517; artifact PDF p. 601","verification_source_id":"passage.catholic-church.missale-romanum.vatican-typica-1962.temporal-s-pii-v-papae-confessoris-orations"},"publication":{"basis":"public-domain","evidence":"The wording is preexisting material of the 1962 typical edition under 17 U.S.C. 103(b), which gives a revised edition no exclusive right in material it did not contribute. The independent registered public-domain witness is the tracked 1862 Pustet text artifact. The position is settled for the repository at src/sources/inventories/missale-romanum-1962-facsimile-rights-v1.toml, which names the seasonal orations as preexisting material and requires exactly this pairing of 103(b) with a public-domain witness.","locator":"Pustet Ratisbon 1862, printed p. 440; text-layer lines 62371-62402; this is the 1862's own entry for the day; artifact rights_status public-domain on a book first published in 1862","source_ids":["artifact.triptych.roman-1962-latin-proper